998 0 obj
  << /ActualText (What we try and do both in my work and in the program at SCI\055Arc is look at the ways that architects can parasitically occupy and operate within the mediums of popular culture\056\nWhen we\047re talking about architecture and fictions\054 I am less talking about speculative architecture projects or the traditions of paper architecture \055 the sort of stuff that students do in kinky design studios at architecture school \055 I\047m talking about architects operating outside of architecture entirely and entering the entertainment industry as directors\054 concept designers or environment designers for films or video games \055 or even in VR\054 advertising or documentary filmmaking\056 I think the days of the speculative architecture project are numbered\054 or at least I see more capacity to affect change by moving outside of our discipline\054 whilst still operating as architects but working specifically through fiction\056 So that\047s what we\047re trying to do\056\0502\051 I try to treat cinema as a kind of site through which to talk about architecture and urban ideas\056\nAnd that\047s what we try to do with our students\056 They don\047t make some crazy speculative building\054 they make a film that is designed for\054 say\054 the Sundance Film Festival or as a pilot for a TV show or a short film that\047s used to pitch for a feature film\056 At the very least it\031s something that is designed for the web to get 100\054000 views\056 That\031s 100\054000 more views than any one architecture project is going to get\056)
     /K [ 15 ]
     /P 999 0 R
     /Pg 968 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

